{"data":{"featured":{"edges":[{"node":{"frontmatter":{"title":"C-Like Compiler","cover":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"constrained","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/51a07/compiler.jpg","srcSet":"/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/5f2c0/compiler.jpg 125w,\n/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/7ac0a/compiler.jpg 250w,\n/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/51a07/compiler.jpg 500w","sizes":"(min-width: 500px) 500px, 100vw"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/4a03a/compiler.avif 125w,\n/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/827c7/compiler.avif 250w,\n/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/60cfc/compiler.avif 500w","type":"image/avif","sizes":"(min-width: 500px) 500px, 100vw"},{"srcSet":"/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/ca6c0/compiler.webp 125w,\n/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/6ef21/compiler.webp 250w,\n/static/9037e09242841eceb38ac0289d17fe06/12849/compiler.webp 500w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"(min-width: 500px) 500px, 100vw"}]},"width":700,"height":525}}},"tech":["OCaml","C++","x86","LLVM"],"github":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/Compiler","external":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/Compiler","cta":null},"html":"<p>A full-stack compiler that translates a C-like imperative language all the way down to optimized x86 assembly code.\nIt was implemented individually in OCaml (with an LLVM-style backend) and comprises all major compiler phases from lexical analysis to code generation.</p>"}},{"node":{"frontmatter":{"title":"Tiny Chat App","cover":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"constrained","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/d40a6/chat_example.png","srcSet":"/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/73682/chat_example.png 175w,\n/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/6a1de/chat_example.png 350w,\n/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/d40a6/chat_example.png 700w","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/1f9df/chat_example.avif 175w,\n/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/fcc08/chat_example.avif 350w,\n/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/678c6/chat_example.avif 700w","type":"image/avif","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"},{"srcSet":"/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/9ab17/chat_example.webp 175w,\n/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/7814d/chat_example.webp 350w,\n/static/008a27471229f52ca019b22ae3d658bc/58f27/chat_example.webp 700w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"}]},"width":700,"height":234}}},"tech":["Erlang","Groovy"],"github":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/CS511-Concurrent-Programming/tree/deploy","external":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/CS511-Concurrent-Programming/tree/deploy","cta":null},"html":"<p>TinyChat is a lightweight, highly concurrent chat application developed in Erlang, showcasing the language's powerful capabilities for building scalable, real-time messaging systems. This project demonstrates Erlang's process model, message passing, and fault tolerance features, making it an excellent portfolio piece for those interested in distributed systems and functional programming.</p>"}},{"node":{"frontmatter":{"title":"Dysfunctionally Functional Language","cover":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"constrained","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/4c030/PL_theory.png","srcSet":"/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/9892c/PL_theory.png 175w,\n/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/b504f/PL_theory.png 350w,\n/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/4c030/PL_theory.png 700w","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/6c37f/PL_theory.avif 175w,\n/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/c6618/PL_theory.avif 350w,\n/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/e6d7c/PL_theory.avif 700w","type":"image/avif","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"},{"srcSet":"/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/0e166/PL_theory.webp 175w,\n/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/674a1/PL_theory.webp 350w,\n/static/63a822835ca409de24eb80c4b3ec257b/f6779/PL_theory.webp 700w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"}]},"width":700,"height":288}}},"tech":["OCaml"],"github":null,"external":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/Principles-Of-Programming/tree/main","cta":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/Principles-Of-Programming/tree/main"},"html":"<p>The project is designed as a Programming Language Theory (PLT) project and incrementally builds up the features of a functional language, starting from arithmetic expressions and evolving towards functions, recursion, references, and type systems.</p>"}},{"node":{"frontmatter":{"title":"Uber Clone","cover":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"constrained","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/75958/uberClone.png","srcSet":"/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/3a042/uberClone.png 175w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/da592/uberClone.png 350w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/75958/uberClone.png 700w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/688e0/uberClone.png 1400w","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/ab4e6/uberClone.avif 175w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/9a969/uberClone.avif 350w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/54a36/uberClone.avif 700w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/a1e99/uberClone.avif 1400w","type":"image/avif","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"},{"srcSet":"/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/32753/uberClone.webp 175w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/4bb4d/uberClone.webp 350w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/1e799/uberClone.webp 700w,\n/static/2a081f21a13b1f14e190eb70af328983/411a5/uberClone.webp 1400w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"(min-width: 700px) 700px, 100vw"}]},"width":700,"height":345}}},"tech":["Javascript","Python","Reactjs","Django & Django Rest Framework","Websockets (Django Channels)","RxJS","Cypress"],"github":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/Ride-Hailing","external":"https://github.com/Chinmay-395/Ride-Hailing","cta":null},"html":"<p>This project is a full-stack ride-hailing application designed to simulate a real-world scenario where users can hail rides and track their routes in real-time. It encompasses a wide range of technologies and methodologies, focusing on real-time communication, authentication, and front-end interactivity. The application is built using Django, Django REST Framework, and Django Channels on the back end, with ReactJS for the front end. It features a RESTful API, token-based authentication with JWTs, real-time data updates via WebSockets, and integration with the Google Maps API.</p>"}}]}}}